      <h1 id="oadp-configuring-using-vmfr" class="group scroll-mt-20">Configure and use OADP VMFR<a href="#oadp-configuring-using-vmfr" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h1>
      <p>Discover VM backups, restore files, and access restored files through a web browser or SSH-based tools.</p>
      <h2 id="oadp-vmfr-enabling_oadp-using-vmfr" class="group scroll-mt-20">Enable OADP VMFR<a href="#oadp-vmfr-enabling_oadp-using-vmfr" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">Enable OADP virtual machine file restore (VMFR) by configuring the <code>DataProtectionApplication</code> (DPA) custom resource (CR) with the <code>vmFileRestore</code> section. You can allow file-level restore operations for VM backups.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You are logged in to the cluster with the <code>cluster-admin</code> role.</li>
      <li>The OADP Operator is installed.</li>
      <li>The <code>DataProtectionApplication</code> (DPA) CR is configured.</li>
      <li>OpenShift Virtualization is installed and running on the cluster.</li>
      <li>You have a default storage class configured on the cluster.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Edit the <code>DataProtectionApplication</code> CR to enable the VMFR feature:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: oadp.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: DataProtectionApplication\nmetadata:\n  name: oadp-backup\n  namespace: openshift-adp\nspec:\n  configuration:\n    nodeAgent:\n      enable: true\n      uploaderType: kopia\n    velero:\n      defaultPlugins:\n        - kubevirt\n        - csi\n        - openshift\n        - aws\n      disableFsBackup: false\n  vmFileRestore:\n    enable: true\n  backupLocations:\n    - velero:\n        config:\n          profile: \"default\"\n          region: <region>\n        provider: aws\n        default: true\n        credential:\n          key: cloud\n          name: <cloud_credentials>\n        objectStorage:\n          bucket: <bucket_name>\n          prefix: velero"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>kubevirt</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the <code>kubevirt</code> Velero plugin in the <code>defaultPlugins</code> list. This plugin is required for VM backup and file-level restore operations.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>vmFileRestore</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the section in the DPA <code>spec</code> to enable the VMFR feature.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>enable</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies whether to enable the VMFR feature. Set to <code>true</code> to enable the feature.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the DPA configuration by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f <dpa_cr_filename>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;dpa_cr_filename&gt;</code> with the file name containing the DPA CR configuration.</p>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      To verify that the DPA is reconciled with the VMFR feature enabled, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get dpa -n openshift-adp -o yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>In the output, verify that the <code>status.conditions</code> section includes a condition with <code>type: VMFileRestoreReady</code> and <code>status: "True"</code>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      To verify that the <code>oadp-vm-file-restore-controller-manager</code> pod is running, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get pod -n openshift-adp"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output should include a running <code>oadp-vm-file-restore-controller-manager</code> pod.</p>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="oadp-vmfr-creating-vmbd_oadp-using-vmfr" class="group scroll-mt-20">Create a VirtualMachineBackupsDiscovery CR<a href="#oadp-vmfr-creating-vmbd_oadp-using-vmfr" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">Create a <code>VirtualMachineBackupsDiscovery</code> (VMBD) custom resource (CR) to identify which Velero backups contain a specified virtual machine (VM). You can locate available backups before performing a file-level restore.</p>
      <p>After you create a VMBD CR, the CR undergoes the following phases:</p>
      <ul>
      <li>The initial phase for the CR is <code>New</code>.</li>
      <li>The controller compiles candidate backups and verifies VM presence in each backup.</li>
      <li>Upon successful discovery, the <code>status.phase</code> field of the VMBD CR is updated to <code>Completed</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>Create all VMFR custom resources in the protected namespace, which is <code>openshift-adp</code> by default.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You are logged in to the cluster with the <code>cluster-admin</code> role.</li>
      <li>You have installed the OADP Operator.</li>
      <li>You have configured the <code>DataProtectionApplication</code> (DPA) CR with the VMFR feature enabled.</li>
      <li>You have existing Velero backups that contain virtual machine data.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create a <code>VirtualMachineBackupsDiscovery</code> CR YAML manifest file with the following configuration:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: oadp.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: VirtualMachineBackupsDiscovery\nmetadata:\n  name: find-my-vm-backups\n  namespace: openshift-adp\nspec:\n  virtualMachineName: \"production-web-server\"\n  virtualMachineNamespace: \"production\""} lang="yaml" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>name</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies a name for the VMBD CR. For example, <code>find-my-vm-backups</code>.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>namespace</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the namespace where the VMBD CR is created. This must be the OADP protected namespace, typically <code>openshift-adp</code>.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>virtualMachineName</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the name of the virtual machine to search for in backups. For example, <code>production-web-server</code>.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>virtualMachineNamespace</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the namespace of the target virtual machine. For example, <code>production</code>.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      </li>
      <li>
      Optional: To filter backups by a time range, add <code>startTime</code> and <code>endTime</code> fields to the <code>spec</code> section:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: oadp.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: VirtualMachineBackupsDiscovery\nmetadata:\n  name: find-my-vm-backups\n  namespace: openshift-adp\nspec:\n  virtualMachineName: \"production-web-server\"\n  virtualMachineNamespace: \"production\"\n  startTime: \"2025-08-01\"\n  endTime: \"2025-09-01\""} lang="yaml" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>startTime</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the start of the time range to filter backups. Backups created before this date are excluded.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>endTime</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the end of the time range to filter backups. Backups created after this date are excluded.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      </li>
      <li>
      Optional: To discover specific backups by name, add the <code>requestedBackups</code> field to the <code>spec</code> section:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: oadp.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: VirtualMachineBackupsDiscovery\nmetadata:\n  name: find-my-vm-backups\n  namespace: openshift-adp\nspec:\n  virtualMachineName: \"production-web-server\"\n  virtualMachineNamespace: \"production\"\n  requestedBackups:\n    - \"initial-backup-from-2024-01-01\"\n    - \"last-working-from-2025-07-28\""} lang="yaml" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>requestedBackups</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies a list of backup names to include in the discovery. These backups are included regardless of any time range filter.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      </li>
      <li>
      To apply the VMBD CR configuration, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f <vmbd_cr_filename>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;vmbd_cr_filename&gt;</code> with the file name containing the VMBD CR configuration.</p>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      To verify that the VMBD CR is successfully created and discovery is complete, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get vmbd find-my-vm-backups -n openshift-adp -o yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: oadp.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: VirtualMachineBackupsDiscovery\nmetadata:\n  name: find-my-vm-backups\n  namespace: openshift-adp\nspec:\n  virtualMachineName: \"production-web-server\"\n  virtualMachineNamespace: \"production\"\nstatus:\n  phase: Completed\n  validBackups:\n    - name: \"backup-2025-09-20\"\n      namespace: openshift-adp\n      createdAt: \"2025-09-20T02:00:00Z\"\n    - name: \"backup-2025-09-15\"\n      namespace: openshift-adp\n      createdAt: \"2025-09-15T02:00:00Z\"\n  backupDiscoveryProgress:\n    - name: \"backup-2025-09-20\"\n      namespace: openshift-adp\n      status: Completed\n      message: \"VM found in backup\"\n      createdAt: \"2025-09-20T02:00:00Z\"\n    - name: \"backup-2025-09-15\"\n      namespace: openshift-adp\n      status: Completed\n      message: \"VM found in backup\"\n      createdAt: \"2025-09-15T02:00:00Z\"\n  conditions:\n    - type: Ready\n      status: \"True\"\n      message: \"Successfully discovered 2 valid backups\"\n      reason: DiscoverySuccessful\n  discoveryStats:\n    totalCandidates: 2\n    completed: 2\n    failed: 0\n    inProgress: 0\n    pending: 0\n    skipped: 0\n  observedGeneration: 1"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>phase: Completed</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies that the discovery process is complete.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>validBackups</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the list of backups that contain the specified virtual machine. Each entry includes the <code>name</code>, <code>namespace</code>, and <code>createdAt</code> timestamp.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>backupDiscoveryProgress</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the discovery progress for each candidate backup, including the <code>status</code> and <code>message</code>.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>discoveryStats</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the total number of candidate backups processed and the count of completed, failed, in-progress, pending, and skipped results.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>observedGeneration</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the last generation value processed by the controller.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      </li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="oadp-vmfr-creating-vmfr_oadp-using-vmfr" class="group scroll-mt-20">Create a VirtualMachineFileRestore CR<a href="#oadp-vmfr-creating-vmfr_oadp-using-vmfr" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">Create a <code>VirtualMachineFileRestore</code> (VMFR) custom resource (CR) to make files from discovered virtual machine (VM) backups accessible for browsing and downloading. You can recover individual files without restoring the entire VM.</p>
      <p>After you create a VMFR CR, the CR undergoes the following phases:</p>
      <ul>
      <li>The initial phase for the CR is <code>New</code>.</li>
      <li>The controller validates the referenced discovery, restores PVCs, and creates file-serving resources.</li>
      <li>Upon successful setup, the <code>status.phase</code> field of the VMFR CR is updated to <code>Completed</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>All VMFR custom resources must be created in the protected namespace, which is <code>openshift-adp</code> by default.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You are logged in to the cluster with the <code>cluster-admin</code> role.</li>
      <li>You have installed the OADP Operator.</li>
      <li>You have configured the <code>DataProtectionApplication</code> (DPA) CR with the VMFR feature enabled.</li>
      <li>You have created a <code>VirtualMachineBackupsDiscovery</code> (VMBD) CR and its <code>status.phase</code> is <code>Completed</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      [Optional] Create a secret containing the credentials for accessing the file browser:
      <p>If you do not create a secret, the VMFR controller creates a secret for you and references it in the VMFR CR.</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: v1\nkind: Secret\nmetadata:\n  name: vmfr-credentials\n  namespace: openshift-adp\ntype: Opaque\ndata:\n  password: <base64_encoded_password>\n  username: <base64_encoded_username>"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>password</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the base64-encoded password for the file browser. The password must be at least 12 characters long before encoding.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>username</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the base64-encoded username for the file browser.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a <code>VirtualMachineFileRestore</code> CR YAML manifest file with the following configuration.
      <p>To configure file browser access:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: oadp.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: VirtualMachineFileRestore\nmetadata:\n  name: restore-config-files\n  namespace: openshift-adp\nspec:\n  backupsDiscoveryRef: find-my-vm-backups\n  selectedBackups:\n    - backup-2025-09-20\n    - backup-2025-09-15\n  fileAccess:\n    fileBrowser:\n      credentialsSecretRef:\n        name: vmfr-credentials\n      exposeExternally: true"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>To configure SSH access:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: oadp.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: VirtualMachineFileRestore\nmetadata:\n  name: restore-config-files\n  namespace: openshift-adp\nspec:\n  backupsDiscoveryRef: find-my-vm-backups\n  selectedBackups:\n    - backup-2025-09-20\n    - backup-2025-09-15\n  fileAccess:\n    ssh: {}"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>name</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies a name for the VMFR CR. For example, <code>restore-config-files</code>.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>namespace</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the namespace where the VMFR CR is created. This must be the OADP protected namespace, typically <code>openshift-adp</code>.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>backupsDiscoveryRef</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the name of the VMBD CR that contains the discovery results. This VMBD CR must be in the <code>Completed</code> phase.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>selectedBackups</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies a list of backup names from the VMBD valid results to restore. A Velero restore operation is created for each backup in this list.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>fileAccess</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the configuration for accessing the restored files.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>fileBrowser</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the file browser configuration for web-based access.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>credentialsSecretRef</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the name of the Kubernetes secret that contains the credentials for file browser access.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>exposeExternally</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies whether to create an external route for accessing the file browser. Set to <code>true</code> to create a publicly accessible route.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>ssh</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the SSH access configuration. Set to <code>{"{"}{"}"}</code> to enable SSH access with autogenerated credentials. The controller generates an SSH key pair and stores it in a Kubernetes secret. Optionally, you can also specify a <code>username</code>, and <code>publickey</code> for enabling the SSH access as shown in the following example:</p></dd>
      </dl>
      <CodeBlock code={"...\n   ssh: \n     username: fedora\n     publicKey: \"\""} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      To apply the VMFR CR configuration, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f <vmfr_cr_filename>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;vmfr_cr_filename&gt;</code> with the file name containing the VMFR CR configuration.</p>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      To verify that the VMFR CR is successfully created and file access is available, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get vmfr restore-config-files -n openshift-adp -o yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: oadp.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: VirtualMachineFileRestore\nmetadata:\n  name: restore-config-files\n  namespace: openshift-adp\nspec:\n  backupsDiscoveryRef: find-my-vm-backups\n  selectedBackups:\n    - backup-2025-09-20\n    - backup-2025-09-15\n  fileAccess:\n    fileBrowser:\n      credentialsSecretRef:\n        name: vmfr-credentials\n      exposeExternally: true\nstatus:\n  phase: Completed\n  fileServingInfo:\n    fileBrowser:\n      clusterAccess: https://restore-config-files-fileserver-svc.production-production-web-server-a1b2c3.svc.cluster.local:8443\n      publicAccess: https://restore-config-files.vmfr.apps.example.com\n      credentialsSecretRef:\n        name: restore-config-files-filebrowser-xk4wm\n        namespace: production-production-web-server-a1b2c3\n  conditions:\n    - type: Ready\n      status: \"True\"\n      message: \"File restore completed, files accessible via file server and external route\"\n      reason: Completed\n    - type: Available\n      status: \"True\"\n      message: \"File server is accessible and serving files\"\n      reason: FileServerAvailable\n  pvcRestores:\n    - pvcName: production-web-server-dv\n      pvcNamespace: production\n      pvcUID: 05ac1521-2a16-4a71-b81f-ccad592b89cd\n      restores:\n        - veleroBackupName: backup-2025-09-20\n          veleroBackupNamespace: openshift-adp\n          veleroRestoreName: vmfr-restore-config-files-backup-2025-09-20-89lfl\n          veleroRestoreNamespace: openshift-adp\n          phase: Completed\n          state: available\n          timestamp: \"2025-09-20T02:00:00Z\"\n        - veleroBackupName: backup-2025-09-15\n          veleroBackupNamespace: openshift-adp\n          veleroRestoreName: vmfr-restore-config-files-backup-2025-09-15-bfqx4\n          veleroRestoreNamespace: openshift-adp\n          phase: Completed\n          state: available\n          timestamp: \"2025-09-15T02:00:00Z\"\n      size: 150Mi\n  createdNamespace: production-production-web-server-a1b2c3\n  observedGeneration: 1"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>phase: Completed</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies that the file restore process is complete and file-serving endpoints are available.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>fileServingInfo.fileBrowser</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the endpoint details and credentials for accessing the restored files through the file browser.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>clusterAccess</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the cluster-internal URL for accessing the file browser.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>publicAccess</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the externally accessible URL for the file browser. This field is present only when <code>exposeExternally</code> is set to <code>true</code>.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>credentialsSecretRef</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the Kubernetes secret that contains the generated credentials for accessing the file browser interface, including the <code>name</code> and <code>namespace</code> of the secret.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>pvcRestores</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the restore status grouped by PVC. Each PVC entry includes the <code>pvcName</code>, <code>pvcNamespace</code>, <code>pvcUID</code>, and a <code>restores</code> list with details for each backup restore.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>state</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the state of the restored PVC. Possible values include <code>available</code>, <code>processing</code>, <code>failed</code>, <code>backup-deleted</code>, <code>backup-missing</code>, <code>unsupported-plugin</code>, and <code>extraction-failed</code>.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>createdNamespace</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the temporary namespace created for hosting the file-serving resources. This namespace is cleaned up when you delete the VMFR CR.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      </li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="oadp-vmfr-accessing-files-web_oadp-using-vmfr" class="group scroll-mt-20">Access restored files through a web browser<a href="#oadp-vmfr-accessing-files-web_oadp-using-vmfr" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">Access restored virtual machine (VM) files through a web browser by using the file browser interface provided by the <code>VirtualMachineFileRestore</code> (VMFR) custom resource (CR). You can browse, preview, and download files from VM backups.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You are logged in to the cluster with the <code>cluster-admin</code> role.</li>
      <li>A <code>VirtualMachineFileRestore</code> (VMFR) CR with the <code>fileAccess.fileBrowser</code> section configured exists.</li>
      <li>The VMFR CR <code>status.phase</code> is <code>Completed</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      To retrieve the file browser access URLs, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get vmfr <vmfr_cr_name> -n openshift-adp -o jsonpath='{.status.fileServingInfo.fileBrowser}'"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;vmfr_cr_name&gt;</code> with the name of the VMFR CR. The output includes the <code>clusterAccess</code> URL for cluster-internal access and the <code>publicAccess</code> URL if <code>exposeExternally</code> is set to <code>true</code>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      If the VMFR CR has <code>exposeExternally</code> set to <code>true</code>, open a web browser and navigate to the <code>publicAccess</code> URL from the status output.
      <p>If <code>exposeExternally</code> is not enabled, set up port forwarding to the file-serving service by running the following command:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc port-forward svc/vmfr-<vmfr_cr_name>-fileserver-svc -n <restore_namespace> 8443:8443"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;vmfr_cr_name&gt;</code> with the name of the VMFR CR and <code>&lt;restore_namespace&gt;</code> with the namespace from the <code>status.createdNamespace</code> field. Then navigate to <code>https://localhost:8443</code> in your web browser.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Log in by using the credentials from the secret you created for file browser access. <strong>Figure 1. OADP VM File Restore Browser login page</strong>
      <BlockImage src="/openshift-docs-markdown/_assets/images/oadp-vmfr-file-browser-login.png" alt="OADP VM File Restore Browser login page" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Browse the files organized by date, backup name, and PVC name. <strong>Figure 2. File browser listing showing backup contents</strong>
      <BlockImage src="/openshift-docs-markdown/_assets/images/oadp-vmfr-file-browser-listing.png" alt="File browser listing showing backup contents" />
      </li>
      <li>
      View the file content by selecting the file. To download a file, select the file and click <strong>Download</strong>. To download a directory as an archive, select the directory and click <strong>Download</strong>. <strong>Figure 3. File preview in the file browser</strong>
      <BlockImage src="/openshift-docs-markdown/_assets/images/oadp-vmfr-file-browser-preview.png" alt="File preview in the file browser" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="oadp-vmfr-accessing-files-ssh_oadp-using-vmfr" class="group scroll-mt-20">Access restored files through SSH<a href="#oadp-vmfr-accessing-files-ssh_oadp-using-vmfr" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">Access restored virtual machine (VM) files through SSH by using <code>rsync</code>, <code>scp</code>, or <code>sftp</code> with the <code>VirtualMachineFileRestore</code> (VMFR) custom resource (CR). You can transfer files from VM backups efficiently.</p>
      <p>When you configure SSH access, the VMFR controller autogenerates an SSH key pair and stores it in a Kubernetes secret. The default SSH username is <code>oadp</code>. The SSH file server listens on port <code>2222</code>.</p>
      <p>The remote path for restored files follows the format <code>/restores/&lt;date&gt;/&lt;backup_name&gt;/&lt;vm_name&gt;/&lt;path_to_file&gt;</code>.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You are logged in to the cluster with the <code>cluster-admin</code> role.</li>
      <li>You have created a <code>VirtualMachineFileRestore</code> (VMFR) CR with the <code>fileAccess.ssh</code> section configured.</li>
      <li>The VMFR CR <code>status.phase</code> is <code>Completed</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      To retrieve the SSH access information, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get vmfr <vmfr_cr_name> -o jsonpath='{.status.fileServingInfo.ssh}' | jq"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;vmfr_cr_name&gt;</code> with the name of the VMFR CR. The output includes the <code>clusterAccess</code> URL and <code>credentialsSecretRef</code> containing the name and namespace of the generated SSH key secret.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Retrieve the private key from the generated secret and save it to a file:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get secret <secret_name> -n <secret_namespace> -o jsonpath='{.data.privateKey}' | base64 -d > id-rsa"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;secret_name&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;secret_namespace&gt;</code> with the values from the <code>status.fileServingInfo.ssh.credentialsSecretRef</code> field.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Set the correct permissions on the private key file:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ chmod 600 id-rsa"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Get the name of the file server service created in the VMFR namespace:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get svc -n <created_namespace> | grep fileserver"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;created_namespace&gt;</code> with the value from the <code>status.createdNamespace</code> field of the VMFR CR.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      To copy a file from the backup by using <code>scp</code>, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ scp -P 2222 -i id-rsa \\\n  -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no \\\n  -o UserKnownHostsFile=/dev/null \\\n  oadp@<fileserver_svc>.<created_namespace>.svc.cluster.local:<remote_path> \\\n  <local_destination>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;fileserver_svc&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the name of the file server service.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;created_namespace&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the namespace from the <code>status.createdNamespace</code> field.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;remote_path&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the path to the file in the format <code>/restores/&lt;date&gt;/&lt;backup_name&gt;/&lt;vm_name&gt;/&lt;path_to_file&gt;</code>.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;local_destination&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the local file path to save the restored file.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      </li>
      <li>
      To start an interactive SFTP session, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ sftp -P 2222 -i id-rsa \\\n  -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no \\\n  oadp@<fileserver_svc>.<created_namespace>.svc.cluster.local"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="oadp-vmfr-deleting-vmfr_oadp-using-vmfr" class="group scroll-mt-20">Delete a VirtualMachineFileRestore CR<a href="#oadp-vmfr-deleting-vmfr_oadp-using-vmfr" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">Delete a <code>VirtualMachineFileRestore</code> (VMFR) custom resource (CR) to clean up file-serving resources after you have recovered the files you need. This helps you free cluster resources used by the file-serving pod and temporary namespace.</p>
      <p>When you delete a VMFR CR, the controller performs the following cleanup operations:</p>
      <ul>
      <li>Stops the file-serving pod and associated services.</li>
      <li>Deletes the restored PVCs.</li>
      <li>Deletes the temporary namespace if one was automatically created.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You are logged in to the cluster with the <code>cluster-admin</code> role.</li>
      <li>You have a <code>VirtualMachineFileRestore</code> CR that you want to delete.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      To delete a <code>VirtualMachineFileRestore</code> CR, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc delete vmfr <vmfr_cr_name> -n openshift-adp"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;vmfr_cr_name&gt;</code> with the name of the VMFR CR.</p>
      </li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      To verify that the VMFR CR is deleted and resources are cleaned up, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get vmfr -n openshift-adp"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The deleted VMFR CR should not appear in the output.</p>
      </li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="oadp-vmfr-end-to-end-workflow_oadp-using-vmfr" class="group scroll-mt-20">Test the VM file restore workflow<a href="#oadp-vmfr-end-to-end-workflow_oadp-using-vmfr" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">Complete an end-to-end workflow that creates a VM, backs it up, and restores individual files through SSH to test the VM file restore feature or verify your configuration.</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You are logged in to the cluster with the <code>cluster-admin</code> role.</li>
      <li>You have installed the OADP Operator.</li>
      <li>OpenShift Virtualization is installed and running on the cluster.</li>
      <li>You have installed the <code>virtctl</code> CLI tool to access the VM.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create a secret for the cloud storage credentials by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create secret generic <secret_name> -n openshift-adp --from-file cloud=<credentials_file_path>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;secret_name&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the name of the cloud credentials secret.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;credentials_file_path&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the path to the file that contains the cloud storage credentials.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a <code>DataProtectionApplication</code> (DPA) CR with the VMFR feature enabled and the <code>kubevirt</code> plugin:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: oadp.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: DataProtectionApplication\nmetadata:\n  name: vmfr-dpa\n  namespace: openshift-adp\nspec:\n  backupLocations:\n    - velero:\n        credential:\n          key: cloud\n          name: <secret_name>\n        default: true\n        objectStorage:\n          bucket: <bucket_name>\n          prefix: velero\n        provider: <provider>\n  configuration:\n    velero:\n      defaultPlugins:\n        - csi\n        - openshift\n        - kubevirt\n        - <provider>\n      disableFsBackup: false\n    nodeAgent:\n      enable: true\n      uploaderType: kopia\n  vmFileRestore:\n    enable: true"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;secret_name&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the name of the cloud credentials secret you created.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;bucket_name&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the name of the object storage bucket.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;provider&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the cloud provider plugin, such as <code>aws</code>, <code>gcp</code>, or <code>azure</code>.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>vmFileRestore</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Enables the VMFR feature by setting the <code>enable</code> field to <code>true</code>.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the DPA configuration by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f <dpa_cr_filename>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify that the DPA is reconciled and the VMFR feature is enabled by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get dpa -n openshift-adp -o yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>In the output, verify that the <code>status.conditions</code> section includes a condition with <code>type: VMFileRestoreReady</code> and <code>status: "True"</code>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify that the <code>velero</code>, <code>nodeAgent</code>, and <code>oadp-vm-file-restore-controller-manager</code> pods are running by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get pod -n openshift-adp"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Generate an SSH key pair for accessing the VM:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/<vm_key_name> -C \"<vm_key_name>\""} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;vm_key_name&gt;</code> with a name for the SSH folder and the key file.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a namespace for the VM by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create ns <vm_namespace>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a Kubernetes secret with the SSH public key in the VM namespace:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create secret generic <ssh_secret_name> \\\n  --from-file=key=<path_to_public_key> \\\n  -n <vm_namespace>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;ssh_secret_name&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the name of the secret that contains the SSH public key.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;path_to_public_key&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the path to the SSH public key file you created in an earlier step. For example, <code>$HOME/.ssh/vm-key.pub</code>.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;vm_namespace&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the namespace for the VM.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a <code>VirtualMachine</code> CR by using the built-in VM template <code>fedora-server-small</code>:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc process -n openshift fedora-server-small -p NAME=<vm_name> | oc apply -n <vm_namespace> -f -"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;vm_name&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the name of the VM.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;vm_namespace&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the namespace for the VM.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      </li>
      <li>
      Wait for the VM to be ready:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc wait --for=condition=Ready vmi/<vm_name> -n <vm_namespace>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Patch the VM configuration <code>accessCredentials</code> object with the SSH public key:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc patch vm <vm_name> -n <vm_namespace> --type=merge -p '{\"spec\":{\"template\":{\"spec\":{\"accessCredentials\":[{\"sshPublicKey\":{\"propagationMethod\":{\"noCloud\":{}},\"source\":{\"secret\":{\"secretName\":\"<ssh_secret_name>\"}}}}]}}}}'"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;vm_name&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the name of the VM.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;vm_namespace&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the namespace for the VM.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;ssh_secret_name&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the name of the secret that contains the SSH public key.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      </li>
      <li>
      SSH to the VM and create a test file:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ virtctl ssh <vm_user>@vmi/<vm_name> \\\n  -n <vm_namespace> \\\n  --identity-file=$HOME/.ssh/<vm_key_name> \\\n  --local-ssh-opts=\"-o StrictHostKeyChecking=no\" \\\n  -c \"echo 'Test file for VMFR validation - $(date)' > /home/fedora/test-vmfr-file.txt\""} lang="terminal" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;vm_user&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the name of the VM user. For the in-built VM template, the user name is <code>fedora</code>.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a <code>Backup</code> CR to back up the VM namespace:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: velero.io/v1\nkind: Backup\nmetadata:\n  name: <backup_name>\n  namespace: openshift-adp\nspec:\n  includedNamespaces:\n    - <vm_namespace>\n  snapshotMoveData: true"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the <code>Backup</code> CR by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f <backup_cr_filename>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify that the backup is complete by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get backup.velero <backup_name> -n openshift-adp -o jsonpath='{.status.phase}'"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output should display <code>Completed</code>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a <code>VirtualMachineBackupsDiscovery</code> CR to identify which backups contain the target VM:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: oadp.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: VirtualMachineBackupsDiscovery\nmetadata:\n  name: <vmbd_name>\n  namespace: openshift-adp\nspec:\n  virtualMachineName: <vm_name>\n  virtualMachineNamespace: <vm_namespace>"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the VMBD CR by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f <vmbd_cr_filename>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify that the discovery is complete by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get vmbd <vmbd_name> -n openshift-adp"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The <code>PHASE</code> column should display <code>Completed</code>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a <code>VirtualMachineFileRestore</code> CR with SSH access to restore files from the discovered backups:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: oadp.openshift.io/v1alpha1\nkind: VirtualMachineFileRestore\nmetadata:\n  name: <vmfr_name>\n  namespace: openshift-adp\nspec:\n  backupsDiscoveryRef: <vmbd_name>\n  fileAccess:\n    ssh: {}"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>The <code>ssh: {"{"}{"}"}</code> configuration instructs the controller to autogenerate an SSH key pair and store it in a Kubernetes secret. The default SSH username is <code>oadp</code>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the VMFR CR by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f <vmfr_cr_filename>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Wait for the VMFR phase to complete:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get vmfr <vmfr_name> -n openshift-adp"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The <code>PHASE</code> column should display <code>Completed</code>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Retrieve the SSH access information by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get vmfr <vmfr_name> -o jsonpath='{.status.fileServingInfo.ssh}' | jq"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The output includes the <code>clusterAccess</code> URL and the <code>credentialsSecretRef</code> containing the name and namespace of the auto generated SSH key secret.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Retrieve the private key from the auto generated secret and save it to a file:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get secret <secret_name> -n <secret_namespace> -o jsonpath='{.data.privateKey}' | base64 -d > id-rsa"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;secret_name&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;secret_namespace&gt;</code> with the values from the <code>status.fileServingInfo.ssh.credentialsSecretRef</code> field.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Copy the private key to the VM so that you can use it for <code>scp</code> from within the VM:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ virtctl scp id-rsa \\\n  <vm_user>@vmi/<vm_name>:/home/<vm_user>/id-rsa \\\n  -n <vm_namespace> \\\n  --identity-file=$HOME/.ssh/<vm_key_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Update the file permissions on the private key inside the VM:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ virtctl ssh <vm_user>@vmi/<vm_name> \\\n  -n <vm_namespace> \\\n  --identity-file=$HOME/.ssh/<vm_key_name> \\\n  --local-ssh-opts=\"-o StrictHostKeyChecking=no\" \\\n  -c \"chmod 600 /home/<vm_user>/id-rsa\""} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Get the name of the file server service created in the VMFR namespace:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ SVC=$(oc get svc -n <created_namespace> | grep fileserver | awk '{print $1}')"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;created_namespace&gt;</code> with the value from the <code>status.createdNamespace</code> field of the VMFR CR.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Restore the file by using <code>scp</code> from within the VM. The remote path format is <code>/restores/&lt;date&gt;/&lt;backup_name&gt;/&lt;vm_name&gt;/&lt;path_to_file&gt;</code>:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ virtctl ssh <vm_user>@vmi/<vm_name> \\\n  -n <vm_namespace> \\\n  --identity-file=$HOME/.ssh/<vm_key_name> \\\n  --local-ssh-opts=\"-o StrictHostKeyChecking=no\" \\\n  -c \"scp -P 2222 \\\n  -i /home/<vm_user>/id-rsa \\\n  -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no \\\n  -o UserKnownHostsFile=/dev/null \\\n  oadp@<fileserver_svc>.<created_namespace>.svc.cluster.local:<remote_path> \\\n  /tmp/restored.txt\""} lang="terminal" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;fileserver_svc&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the name of the file server service you retrieved in an earlier step.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;created_namespace&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the namespace from the <code>status.createdNamespace</code> field.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;remote_path&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the path to the file in the format <code>/restores/&lt;date&gt;/&lt;backup_name&gt;/&lt;vm_name&gt;/&lt;path_to_file&gt;</code>. For example, <code>"/restores/2026-02-10/test-backup/fedora-vm-test/home/fedora/test-vmfr-file.txt"</code></p></dd>
      </dl>
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify that the restored file is intact by comparing MD5 checksums:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ virtctl ssh <vm_user>@vmi/<vm_name> \\\n  -n <vm_namespace> \\\n  --identity-file=$HOME/.ssh/<vm_key_name> \\\n  --local-ssh-opts=\"-o StrictHostKeyChecking=no\" \\\n  -c \"echo '===MD5 Checksums===' && md5sum /home/fedora/test-vmfr-file.txt /tmp/restored.txt\""} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The checksums of the original and restored files should match. You should see an output as shown in the following example:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"===MD5 Checksums===\n92245490c552e83baf5f2a2e898d9fff  /home/fedora/test-vmfr-file.txt\n92245490c552e83baf5f2a2e898d9fff  /tmp/restored.txt"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      After you have recovered the files, delete the VMFR CR to clean up resources:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc delete vmfr <vmfr_name> -n openshift-adp"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
    </article>
